## Read coverage in three parts

| Part | Evidence required |
| --- | --- |
| Native capability | Official Home Assistant documentation or source shows that the feature exists |
| civica.uk implementation | A named maintained component or bounded adapter implements a change; theme variables alone are a narrower claim |
| Design specification | The guide explains capability, state, content, accessibility and failure behaviour, with task-specific acceptance checks |

The current companion adapts known shell hosts, profile colour settings, numeric attention badges, seven native Energy graph types and three named household cards. It also supplies action links. The maintained lighting card adds its own room and scene controls. Other native surfaces retain their own behaviour and the styles they expose. See [Compatibility and limits](../get-started/compatibility.html) for the exact boundary.

## Priority and dependencies

**P1** means the specification is needed before claiming reliable coverage of consequential actions, permissions, privacy, recovery or task accessibility. **P2** covers the remaining work needed for coherent household use. These are design-work priorities, not a claim that every native surface has a confirmed defect.

Start with the shared state and action contracts, then the domain controls and flows that depend on them. Develop native task examples from actual capabilities and synthetic data. Keep a supported native route available while a new recipe is specified and tested.

The [machine-readable gap register](../reference/ha-component-gaps.json) contains exact file and line evidence, source records, dependencies, rule-file hashes and acceptance criteria. The [component organisation review](component-audit.html) identifies where shared rules should live.

